 - A patch that is not an array, or that has a hole in it, is rejected with a `JsonPatchError`.
 
+## JSON Merge Patch
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies a JSON Merge Patch ([RFC 7386](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386)), a partial
+JSON document, to a target and returns the merged document. It is the simpler format our clients use for partial
+updates, where a JSON Patch would need a list of operations:
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+const doc = { order: { items: ["A-1"], note: "rush", gift: true } };
+applyMergePatch(doc, { order: { note: null, items: ["B-2"] } });
+// { order: { items: ["B-2"], gift: true } }
+```
+
+- An object patch merges into the target member by member. A member whose value is `null` removes the member of the
+  target; any other member is merged recursively. Arrays are replaced whole, never merged element by element.
+- A patch that is not an object replaces the whole target. An object patch applied to a target that is not an object,
+  including an array, starts from an empty object.
+- The input is never mutated, and the result shares no structure with the target or the patch.
+- A patch that is not a JSON value, at any depth, is rejected with a `JsonMergePatchError`, which is exported too.
+- A merge patch cannot set a member to `null`, because `null` means removal, and it cannot address array elements.
+  Use `applyPatch` for those cases.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test`.
